title:: Evaluating and Executing .scd Files (SC-IDE & CLI)
summary:: Guide for startup, evaluating, loading, and executing .scd files in SC-IDE and from the command line
categories:: Tutorials
related:: Classes/Document, Classes/Main#thisProcess, Classes/PathName, Classes/Platform, Classes/File, Classes/Interpreter

DESCRIPTION::
This guide summarizes options for starting, evaluating, loading, and executing teletype::.scd:: files within SC-IDE and from the command line.
It also covers techniques for modularizing code with relative paths and for automatic execution upon file opening or SuperCollider startup.

table::
## Option || Use Case || Notes
## teletype::startup.scd:: || Global Boot || Runs every time

SuperCollider starts
## code::/*RUN*/::

at the first line of a teletype::.scd:: file || Auto-Run on Open || SC-IDE specific:

runs code when file opens
## code::.load:: || Execute File || Standard way

to execute a full teletype::.scd:: file

without opening it
## code::.loadRelative:: || Execute File || Best for portable projects
## code::thisProcess.interpreter.executeFile(path ... args):: || Execute File

with Arguments || Standard way

to execute a full teletype::.scd:: file

without opening it

with arguments
## Document.open || Open in Editor || Opens file in IDE

(does not run code)
## CLI (Command-Line Interface) || Batch/Headless || Runs

in the terminal

(macOS/Linux) or

in the Command Prompt

(Windows)
::

strong::Common Pitfalls::


list::
## strong::Document Path::

code::resolveRelative:: and code::loadRelative:: will return code::nil:: or print code::WARNING:: if the current document has not been saved, as it does not yet have a path on disk.

## strong::Infinite Loops::

Be cautious when using code::.load:: in a way that causes a file to load itself (directly or indirectly), which can lead to infinite loops.
::


section:: Startup File (Global Auto-Run)

To run code automatically whenever SuperCollider launches (regardless of which file is opened), you can use the teletype::startup.scd:: file in code::Platform.userAppSupportDir::.
In SC-IDE, you can also open it directly from the menu bar by selecting teletype::File:: → teletype::Open startup file::.
If you are not using SC-IDE, follow these steps instead:

numberedlist::
## Find where your startup file is located:

code::
Platform.userAppSupportDir.openOS
::

## Open the startup file for editing (create the file if it doesn't exist):

code::
(
var path = Platform.userAppSupportDir +/+ "startup.scd";
if(File.exists(path).not) { File.use(path, "w") };
path.openDocument
)
::
::

Examples of useful startup code (inside teletype::startup.scd::):

list::
## To load other files:

code::
"~/Documents/SuperColliderProject1/main.scd".standardizePath.load;
"~/Documents/SuperColliderProject2/main.scd".standardizePath.load;
::
## To set the number of input/output bus channels:

code::
s.options.numInputBusChannels = ~nI = 8;
s.options.numOutputBusChannels = ~nO = 8;
::
## To set memory size:

code::
s.options.memSize = 2.pow(21);
::
## To set audio device:

code::
s.options.device = "Fireface 800 (3F0)";
::

note::
Replace teletype::Fireface 800 (3F0):: with the exact audio interface name reported by sclang.

table::
## 💡 Tip:

List available audio interface names:


list::
## List all audio interface names:

code::
ServerOptions.devices        // Enumerates all audio devices

// Prints each name on a separate line:
ServerOptions.devices.do { |device| device.postln }
::

## List audio input device names:

code::
// Enumerates input devices only:
ServerOptions.inDevices

// Prints each name on a separate line:
ServerOptions.inDevices.do { |device| device.postln }
::

## List audio output device names:

code::
// Enumerates output devices only:
ServerOptions.outDevices

// Prints each name on a separate line:
ServerOptions.outDevices.do { |device| device.postln }
::
::
::
::
::


section:: Auto-Run on Document Open (SC-IDE)

In SC-IDE, you can make a specific file execute automatically when it is opened.
To do this, you can place he string code::/*RUN*/:: on the first line of the document.

code::
/*RUN*/
"Hello, this scd file runs automatically when opened".postln;
s.boot
::

note::

list::
## This feature is specific to the SC-IDE editor.
It is useful for performance patches or tools that should start immediately when the file is opened.

## If this does not work, follow these steps to resolve the issue:

numberedlist::
## (Optional) Check the value of code::Document.autoRun:::

code::
Document.autoRun
::
The default value is teletype::true::, but it may have been changed to teletype::false:: by evaluating code.
## Set the value of code::Document.autoRun:: to teletype::true:::

code::
Document.autoRun_(true)
::
## (Optional) Verify the value of code::Document.autoRun:: again:

code::
Document.autoRun
::
::
::
::

section:: .load and .loadRelative (Executing .scd files without arguments)

This is the most common way to execute code stored in other files from within a running program.

warning::Relative paths work only if the current file has been saved to disk.::


list::
## Load using absolute path

code::
"/full/path/to/filename.scd".load
::
## Load using relative paths (Recommended for projects)

code::
"filename.scd".loadRelative // Searches in the same directory
::

code::
"../lib/utils.scd".loadRelative // Searches in the parent/lib directory
::
## Alternative relative path construction (Manual)

code::
(
var path = thisProcess.nowExecutingPath.dirname;
(path ++ "modules/synthdefs.scd").load
)
::
::

note:: Variable Scope in Loaded Files

When working across multiple teletype::.scd:: files, it helps to use these variable types with the correct scope:


list::
## strong::Environment variables:: (e.g., ~myVar) and strong::interpreter variables:: (e.g., a) persist and are shared between the main file and any files it loads.
## strong::Local variables:: (e.g., var myVar) are scoped only to the file being loaded and are not visible to the main document.
::

code::
// in main.scd
~globalSetting = 100;
"config.scd".loadRelative;
~loadedSetting.postln      // Works if defined in config.scd

// in config.scd
var localCalc = 10 * ~globalSetting; // uses ~globalSetting defined in main.scd for calculation
~loadedSetting = localCalc           // exports result to environment
::
::

section:: thisProcess.interpreter.executeFile (Execution with Arguments)

You can use this to execute a file with arguments.
Note that the teletype::.scd:: file is treated like a function body—do not wrap the file in curly braces.
For example, to generate sound with a variable channel count, save the following as teletype::sound.scd:::

code::
|numchans = 0|
x = { |spread = 1, level = 0.2, center = 0.0|
	Splay.ar(
		SinOsc.ar({ |i| LFNoise2.kr(1).exprange(200, 4000) } ! numchans),
		spread,
		level,
		center
	)
}.play
::

Then, from another teletype::.scd:: file in the same folder, you can run:

code::
x = thisProcess.interpreter.executeFile("sound.scd".resolveRelative, 10)
x.free

x = thisProcess.interpreter.executeFile("sound.scd".resolveRelative, 100)
x.free
::

section:: Opening scd Files in SC-IDE

You can use any of the following methods to open in the text editor without evaluating its code immediately.


list::
## Open using absolute path:

code::
Document.open("/full/path/to/filename.scd")
::
or

code::
"/full/path/to/filename.scd".openDocument
::

## Open using a relative path (you must save the current file first):

code::
// Opens a document located in the same folder as the file containing the evaluated code:
"filename.scd".resolveRelative.openDocument
::

code::
// Opens a document located in the parent folder of the file containing the evaluated code:
"../other.scd".resolveRelative.openDocument
::
::

section:: Execution from the Command Line

SuperCollider (sclang) can be run from a terminal or command prompt.
This is useful for installations, headless systems (e.g., Raspberry Pi), or batch processing.


list::
## Basic usage:

code::
sclang filename.scd        // Linux and macOS
sclang.exe filename.scd    // Windows: press CTRL + SHIFT + 2 after launching the terminal
::

To stop execution, you can use the following keyboard shortcuts:

table::
## OS || Linux || macOS || Windows
## Keyboard shortcuts || teletype::CTRL + C:: || teletype::COMMAND + .:: || teletype::CTRL + C::
::

table::
## 💡 Tip:


list::
## To run code::sclang:: or code::sclang.exe:: directly, the path to code::sclang:: must be added to your system PATH environment variable.


list::
## strong::macOS::

Add the following line to the shell configuration file of the operating system (teletype::~/.zshrc::):

code::
export PATH="/Applications/SuperCollider.app/Contents/MacOS:$PATH"
::

note::
If SuperCollider is installed elsewhere, replace teletype::/Applications/SuperCollider.app:: with your actual installation path.
::

Make sure you save the teletype::~/.zshrc:: file after adding the line above.
Close any open terminal windows, then open a new one to apply the change.

table::
## The file teletype::~/.zshrc:: does not appear in Finder on macOS by default because it is a hidden system file.
On macOS, pressing teletype::COMMAND + SHIFT + .:: in a Finder window toggles the visibility of all hidden files.
You can open the file by double‑clicking it with the system’s default application, or by choosing your preferred application via the teletype::Open With:: option in the context menu (which appears when you right‑click the file).
Advanced users familiar with terminal commands and the nano editor can edit the file directly by running teletype::nano ~/.zshrc::.
::

## strong::Windows::

On Windows, the installation folder does not appear in your PATH environment variable by default.
To add it, you can open a Command Prompt and run:

code::
setx PATH "%PATH%;C:\Program Files\SuperCollider-3.x.x\"
::

note::
Replace teletype::C:\Program Files\SuperCollider-3.x.x\ :: with your actual SuperCollider installation path.
::

Close and reopen Command Prompt (or open a new terminal window or open a new tab by pressing teletype::CTRL + SHIFT + 2::).
The folder will now be included in your PATH, and you can run SuperCollider from any terminal location.

## strong::Linux::
On Linux, SuperCollider is usually installed in teletype::/usr/bin/:: or teletype::/usr/local/bin/:: by default, and it is usually available on your PATH automatically.
::

## To run a file by name only (without specifying the full path), the CLI command must be executed from the same folder as the file.
::
::
## With configuration file:

code::
sclang -l sclang_conf.yaml filename.scd     // Linux and macOS
sclang.exe -l sclang_conf.yaml filename.scd // Windows
::

table::
## 💡 Tip:

The path to teletype::sclang_conf.yaml:: must be explicitly specified if the command is executed outside of code::Platform.userConfigDir::.
::
::

section:: Examples

subsection:: Example 1: Auto-running installation file

numberedlist::
## Save a teletype::.scd:: file containing the following code:

code::
/*RUN*/
s.waitForBoot({
    "Installation Started".postln;
    { SinOsc.ar(440, 0, 0.1) }.play
})
::
## Open the file using SC-IDE.
## All code in the file is evaluated as soon as it opens.
::

subsection:: Example 2: Project Loader Script without arguments (main.scd)

Assume the referenced files are in the same folder as this teletype::.scd:: file:

code::
(
// Load modules relative to this file
"audio/synths.scd".loadRelative;
"music/patterns.scd".loadRelative;
"interface/gui.scd".loadRelative
)
::

subsection:: Example 3: Project Loader Script with arguments (main.scd and the .scd file being executed)

numberedlist::
## Save the following code as teletype::monitorGUI.scd:: in the teletype::~/Downloads/:: folder:

code::
|numIns=2, numOuts=2|
var height = Window.availableBounds.height;
var serverMeterViewWidth = ServerMeterView.getWidth(numIns, numOuts);
var rect = Rect(0, 0, 255 + serverMeterViewWidth, height);
var window = Window("server tools", rect);
var bounds = window.bounds;
var serverMeterView = View(window, bounds.width @ (ServerMeterView.height * 2));
var plotTreeViewView = View(window,
	Rect(
		0,
		ServerMeterView.height * 2,
		bounds.width,
		bounds.height - (ServerMeterView.height * 2)
	)
);
ServerMeterView(s, serverMeterView, 0@0, numIns, numOuts);
Stethoscope(s, numOuts, view: UserView(serverMeterView,
	Rect(
		serverMeterViewWidth,
		0,
		bounds.bounds.width - serverMeterViewWidth,
		serverMeterView.bounds.height
	)
));
FreqScopeView(serverMeterView, Rect(
	3,
	25 + ServerMeterView.height,
	bounds.bounds.width - 6,
	ServerMeterView.height - 10
)).active_(true).freqMode_(1);
s.plotTreeView(0.5, plotTreeViewView);
window.front
::

## Add the following code to your teletype::startup.scd:: file:

code::
thisProcess.interpreter.executeFile("~/Downloads/monitorGUI.scd".standardizePath, 2, 4);
::
## Recompile the class library or restart SuperCollider.
::

subsection:: Example 4: Headless execution (Shell Script)

warning::
The path to sclang must be registered in the operating system’s terminal/command‑line environment so that it is recognized as a valid command.
::

note::
The directive code::/*RUN*/:: in the first line of scd files does not work in the CLI environment.
::

subsubsection:: Linux and macOS

If the filename is teletype::run.sh::, open a terminal in the same folder and run:

code::
./run.sh
::
teletype::run.sh:: should contain:

code::
#!/bin/bash
cd "$(dirname "$0")"
sclang installation.scd
::

table::
## 💡 Tip:

After creating teletype::run.sh:: file, run the following command from the same folder in a terminal window:

code::
chmod +x ./run.sh
::
::

subsubsection:: Windows

If the filename is teletype::run.bat::, open a Command Prompt in the same folder and run:

code::
run.bat
::

teletype::run.sh:: should contain:

code::
@echo off
cd /d %~dp0
sclang installation.scd
::
